ble thanks to a 20,000 pound  grant from the community initiative  foundation 7,000 pound from the Essex  Community Foundation 1500 pound from  Rochford district council plus capital  funding from stanbridge parish council  who are the sole trustees of the  memorial hall residents could work  together to restore their hoard to its  original beauty making it the heart of  the community once more stanbridge  memorial hall now houses many local  groups including mini marvels preschool  disability sport and the Stanbridge  fishery program and welcomes people and  groups from across the district to use  its facilities the First Parish summit  has been hosted by Rochford District  Council as part of efforts to work  together in the interests of residents  the event was organized by the council's  economic development team to encourage  better communication between the  district parish and town councils so  that we can work more closely together  it also provided a forum for councils to  learn from each other's experience to  the benefit of all those who live and  work in the district delegates heard  from guest speakers including Essex  Police RSPB and Rochford  council's planning and economic  development teams workshops included  communication and training emergency  planning Environmental Services  initiatives and funding local highways  neighborhood planning groups and tourism  visit your local market this spring with  a friendly bustling atmosphere and huge  variety of affordable goods on offer  it's well worth visiting your local  market this spring the district's weekly  markets feature a wide variety of stalls  including fruit and veg clothing and  household products Rockford mark pitts  meets every tuesday in rochford square  Raley market meets every Wednesday along  Raley high street and for something a  bit different rochford farmers market  meets on the first Saturday of every  month in the WI hall 930 a.m. till  midday selling quality local produce  rochford District Council has planted  trees at Cherry Orchard Jubilee country  park in remembrance of those who fought  in the First World War one of the many  commemoration events been organized  around the district the tree planting  was a unique and special opportunity to  leave a long lasting legacy of the brave  First World War servicemen 420 tree  saplings had been donated by the  Woodland Trust which have been planted  as an avenue to represent the location  of the key battles throughout the war on  this front after the saplings were  planted John Baker chair of  southend-on-sea branch of the Western  Front Association that a small ceremony  of remembrance which was followed by a  minute's silence mr. Baker said I would  like to thank the woodland trust and rot  your district council for supporting my  project to create a living memorial to  all those who served and died during the  First World War the tree-lined path has  been laid out in the shape of the  Western Front that stretched for over  450 miles from the Belgian coast to the  border with Switzerland each tree  represents a man from the borough who  died in the conflict and the two lines  of trees represent the opposing  frontline trenches as people walk along  the path I hope they will take time to  reflect on all those who  made the supreme sacrifice for their  country the council is also proud to  publish a record to remember the  sacrifice of more than 400 local men who  died in the First World War the list  details the name of each man unit or  ship in which he served date he died and  where he is buried or the memorial where  he is remembered it could be accessed at  wwh Fujio UK forward slash ww1 the list  was compiled as a personal project by  Rochford district council employee jim  Cavani who is a member of the Western  Front Association he had the sistance of  mike davis and john priestly are rarely  through the looking glass if you want to  try their website it's w WR le history  co uk fear irvin 